# A generic call's argument is a value like any other: a name that resolves to
# nothing is reported where it is written. The generic leg used to walk its
# arguments with no unresolved-value check at all, so the name reached the
# emitter, which wrote it into C verbatim for the C compiler to reject with
# `'nosuchname' undeclared`. The concrete argument walk has always reported it.
main: function is {
    buf: "x".string
    buf.append nosuchname
    print nomessage
    print "\{buf}"
}
